                {'stem': 'GVF is commonly used for follow-up in glaucoma and:', 'options': ['Neuro-ophthalmology', 'Dry eye', 'Refraction for glasses', 'Cataract grading'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'theory', 'explanation': 'GVF is valuable in glaucoma and neuro-ophthalmology, where full-field mapping aids diagnosis and monitoring.', 'reasoning_eligible': False},
                {'stem': 'During GVF the patient must keep steadily fixating the central target so that:', 'options': ['Each plotted point maps to a known retinal location', 'The stimulus can be moved faster across the bowl', 'The pressure inside the eye drops while the test runs', 'The chart prints itself at the end of the test'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'practical', 'explanation': 'Stable fixation is essential for the operator to map each response to the correct field position.', 'reasoning_eligible': False},
                {'stem': 'A larger, brighter Goldmann stimulus (e.g. V4e vs I2e) will map:', 'options': ['A larger isopter further out in the field', 'A smaller isopter closer to the fixation point', 'Nothing at all, no matter how bright it is', 'The eye pressure rather than the field'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'theory', 'explanation': 'Bigger/brighter targets are detected further out, producing larger isopters; smaller/dimmer ones map inner isopters.', 'reasoning_eligible': False},
