                {'stem': 'An elderly patient with very advanced glaucoma has only a small central island left. Why might GVF be preferred over another HVF?', 'options': ['Kinetic GVF can map the residual peripheral island', 'GVF measures the eye pressure rather than the field', 'HVF cannot be used to test for glaucoma at all', 'GVF is always faster and needs no operator skill'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'situational', 'explanation': 'GVF maps remaining peripheral vision and its interactive style suits patients who cannot sustain the automated test.', 'reasoning_eligible': True},
                {'stem': "A neuro patient's GVF shows loss on the same side in BOTH eyes respecting the vertical midline. This pattern suggests:", 'options': ['A homonymous defect behind the chiasm', 'Glaucoma affecting both optic nerve heads', 'A cataract clouding the vision in both eyes', 'A normal field with no real defect present'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'situational', 'explanation': "Homonymous, vertical-midline-respecting loss localises behind the chiasm; GVF's full-field map helps reveal it.", 'reasoning_eligible': True},
                {'stem': "Why must the SAME operator technique and stimulus settings be used for a patient's serial GVF comparison?", 'options': ['Operator technique itself can mimic a real change', 'Any technique at all gives exactly the same field map', 'A change in technique changes the diagnosis itself', 'Technique only affects the way the chart is printed'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'situational', 'explanation': 'Because results vary with technique, consistent stimulus and method let true progression be distinguished from operator variation.', 'reasoning_eligible': True},
                {'stem': 'A patient with a drooping upper lid shows an artefactual superior isopter defect. The OT should:', 'options': ['Lift or tape the lid clear and re-plot that area', 'Record the finding as genuine superior field loss', 'Ignore the superior field and carry on plotting', 'Abandon the test and book the patient again'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'situational', 'explanation': 'A ptotic lid blocks superior targets, mimicking loss; taping the lid removes the artefact, as in automated fields.', 'reasoning_eligible': True},
                {'stem': 'The correct near trial lens is used for the CENTRAL isopters but removed for the periphery because:', 'options': ['The lens rim would block peripheral targets', 'Peripheral vision needs no focusing at all', 'The trial lens alters the pressure reading', 'The lens improves peripheral detection'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'practical', 'explanation': 'The trial-lens rim occludes peripheral stimuli, so it is used for central testing and removed when plotting the periphery.', 'reasoning_eligible': True},
                {'stem': 'Progressive SHRINKAGE of the isopters over visits (a constricting field) can indicate conditions such as:', 'options': ['Retinitis pigmentosa or advancing glaucoma', 'Vision that is steadily improving over time', 'A need for a new pair of reading glasses', 'Nothing of any clinical importance at all'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'situational', 'explanation': 'Constricting isopters (tunnel vision) occur in retinitis pigmentosa and advanced glaucoma and should be flagged.', 'reasoning_eligible': True},
                {'stem': 'During GVF the operator monitors fixation, partly by occasionally presenting a stimulus in the:', 'options': ['Blind spot, where a response means lost fixation', 'Far periphery only, well outside all of the isopters', "Patient's ear, to check that they are still awake", 'Central 1 degree only, right on the fixation point'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'practical', 'explanation': "A 'seen' target placed in the physiological blind spot means the eye has moved off fixation, prompting re-instruction.", 'reasoning_eligible': True},
                {'stem': 'Why is patient instruction and rapport especially important in GVF?', 'options': ['The patient must signal the moment they first see it', 'The machine does all of the work automatically anyway', 'Rapport has no real effect on the field that is plotted', 'Only the trial lens power matters to the result'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'situational', 'explanation': 'Kinetic perimetry depends on prompt, honest responses as the target appears; good instruction improves accuracy.', 'reasoning_eligible': True},
                {'stem': 'A patient fatigues during a long GVF. A sensible OT action is to:', 'options': ['Offer a brief rest, then resume the test', 'Push on without stopping until it is done', 'Speed the stimulus up to finish more quickly', 'Record the incomplete field as the result'], 'correct': [0], 'qtype': 'single', 'kind': 'situational', 'explanation': 'Fatigue degrades kinetic responses; a short rest helps the patient respond reliably for a trustworthy map.', 'reasoning_eligible': True},
